WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:05.180
Sequels: Hollywood loves them, and film fans
love to complain about them.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.340
With studios desperate to squeeze even the
basest of horror concepts for one more curdling

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.540
scare, Hollywood's sequelitis has run as rampant
in the horror genre as any other.

00:00:14.549 --> 00:00:18.860
But not every horror tale is worthy of a sequel,
and most sequels fail to live up to the films

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:19.860
that inspired them.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.430
Of course, there have been a few diamonds
found in the mangled rough of horror

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:27.720
flick sequels, so let's have a look at some
of the all-time greats.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.820
Scream 2

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:34.460
Re-teaming the cast and crew from 1996's surprise
smash hit Scream, Wes Craven and company basically

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.430
recycled the same formula for Scream 2, and
that meta mix of comedy, mystery, and terror

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.930
is just as fruitful the second time around.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:46.310
It helps that screenwriter Kevin Williamson,
exceedingly well versed in horror movie lore,

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:50.150
crafted another blisteringly original screenplay
for his Scream follow-up.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.180
"Many sequels have surpassed their original."

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.180
"Oh yeah?"

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.550
It succinctly lays out the rules for a horror
movie before giddily following them to a "T."

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.820
The result is a film that knows when to make
you laugh, when to make you cringe, and yes,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.220
when to make you scream.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.869
If only every sequel aspired to as much.

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:07.869
Aliens

00:01:07.869 --> 00:01:12.421
When Ridley Scott brought his space-set chiller
Alien to the big screen in 1979, the film

00:01:12.421 --> 00:01:16.200
quite literally changed the definition of
what a horror movie could be.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.569
Seven years later, fresh off the success of
Terminator, cocky young director James Cameron

00:01:20.569 --> 00:01:24.479
released his hotly anticipated sequel, simply
titled Aliens.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:28.340
To the surprise of fans and critics alike,
the film packed just as visceral a punch as

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.539
its predecessor, often even eclipsing Scott's
film in terms of energy, effects, and unbridled

00:01:33.539 --> 00:01:34.539
terror.

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:38.990
Whether or not you prefer Scott's claustrophobic
slow burn approach to Cameron's action-packed,

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:43.060
creature-heavy confection, Aliens is still
widely viewed not just as one of the greatest

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.659
horror sequels ever made, but one of the best
sequels ever produced — period.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:51.720
(the greatest movie line ever)

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.759
Friday the 13th Part 2

00:01:54.759 --> 00:01:58.979
While few would argue the merits of Friday
the 13th as great cinema, even its biggest

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:03.109
detractors have to acknowledge the film's
merits as a great slasher flick that scared

00:02:03.109 --> 00:02:05.859
up some unexpectedly impressive box office.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:09.970
It came as no surprise, then, that the film's
producers wanted to revisit the formula in

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.850
the form of a direct sequel.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:15.879
What was surprising was that they actually
made a better movie the second time around.

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:20.010
Make no mistake, Friday the 13th Part 2 is
one of those rare sequels that actually is

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.330
better than the original, even if its setup
and execution are almost identical to the

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.330
original.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:29.480
It doubles down on the action, resulting in
a film that feels bigger, bolder, and scarier

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.599
than the original.

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:34.629
It also happens to feature the debut of one
of slasherdom's biggest baddies, the one and

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:37.230
only Jason Voorhees.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.849
(jump scare)

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:41.189
Dawn of the Dead

00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:46.360
Filmmaker George A. Romero essentially invented
the zombie genre with his 1968 feature film

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.220
debut Night of the Living Dead.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.329
Little did we know, Romero was just scratching
the surface.

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:56.000
When he returned to the genre with 1978's
Dawn of the Dead, viewers were subjected to

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.500
the full force of Romero's caustic vision
for hell on earth with a sequel that included

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.189
twice as much gore and a far sharper
satirical edge.

00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:08.769
This perfect storm of zombie fiction is as
shocking to the eyes as it is to the intellect,

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:13.120
and remains one of the greatest zombie movies,
sequel or otherwise, in history.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.329
Wes Craven's New Nightmare

00:03:14.329 --> 00:03:19.080
A Nightmare on Elm Street is one of the greatest
horror movies ever made, but the same sadly

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.280
can't be said for the string of increasingly
inferior sequels that followed it.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.030
It came as a bit of a surprise, then, when
Craven himself — who hadn't directed for

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.290
the franchise since the original — announced
his return to Elm Street with 1994's Wes Craven's

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.420
New Nightmare.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.170
That surprise turned to outright shock when
New Nightmare turned out to be the smartest

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.640
movie in the franchise.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.030
The film takes a daringly meta approach, with
the formerly dream-stalking Freddy Krueger

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.609
terrorizing the real-life cast and crew of
the original film in the real world.

00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:52.109
New Nightmare sort of has no business being
as good or as smart as it is, yet it's still

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:54.269
easily the second-best film in the series.

00:03:54.269 --> 00:03:56.370
The Devil's Rejects

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.500
Rob Zombie built a reputation as one of the
more intriguing directors working in the horror

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.950
genre on the strength of his debut film, the
sadistically stylized House of 1000 Corpses...

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.900
and its equally stylish sequel, The Devil's
Rejects.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.980
Behind a sick classic rock soundtrack and
a razor-sharp screenplay that knows when to

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:18.090
shock and when to pull a laugh, The Devil's
Rejects is part road movie, part revenge thriller,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:22.650
and part gory grindhouse extravaganza that
surpasses its predecessor in almost every

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:23.780
way.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.080
Evil Dead 2

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:30.280
On paper, Sam Raimi's giddily gory classic
Evil Dead didn't exactly scream for a sequel,

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.360
even if it was sort of open for one.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.840
Evil Dead 2 is essentially the exact same
story as the original, and it's just as gory

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.630
and grueling as you'd expect.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:43.350
It's also hysterically funny this go-round
with Raimi's deliciously D.I.Y. aesthetic

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.990
delivering ghoulish gasps and guffaws.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.750
You could say they just don't make 'em like
this anymore, but then again, no one besides

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:55.080
Raimi ever really did, and Evil Dead 2 is
his one unmitigated masterpiece.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.860
"No!!!!"

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.950
"Yes!"

00:04:58.950 --> 00:04:59.950
Thanks for watching!

